Primary driver for all three: Kim SH, Ziegelmayer S, Busch F, Mertens CJ, Keicher M, Adams LC,
Bressem KK, Braren R, Makowski MR, Kirschke JS, Hedderich DM, Wiestler B. "Exposing the fragility
of LLM reasoning through bias-inducing prompts: evidence from BiasMedQA." BMJ Digit Health AI
2026;2(1):e000189. doi 10.1136/bmjdhai-2025-000189, PMID 42712292, PMC13528315.
Peer-reviewed; verified against PubMed metadata 10 Sep 2026. Klinikum rechts der Isar /
Technical University of Munich.

Design: 1,273 clinical case vignettes from the public BiasMedQA dataset covering seven established
cognitive biases. Three model families — Llama-3.3-70B, Qwen3-32B, Gemini-2.5-Flash — each tested
against its own reasoning-enhanced variant, under three prompt conditions (base, debiasing,
few-shot). Two mixed-effects logistic regression models fitted per model pair. Gemini-2.5-Flash
additionally tested on four bias-inducing prompts written for the study and never published.

Two findings carry:
  (i) Reasoning did not consistently reduce bias susceptibility. In Llama-3.3-70B and
      Gemini-2.5-Flash it AMPLIFIED vulnerability to several bias-inducing prompts; in Qwen3-32B it
      reduced susceptibility to one of seven. Authors' conclusion: "In none of the three LLMs,
      reasoning was able to consistently reduce vulnerability to bias-inducing prompts."
  (ii) Published vs unpublished prompt contrast. Gemini-2.5-Flash scored 80.0-88.6% on the
      published set and 47.4-86.1% on the four unpublished prompts — read by the authors as
      "potential contamination of its training data and exposing underlying brittleness."

Debiasing and few-shot prompting produced statistically significant reductions in biased responses
across all three architectures.

