EDIT 1 — SPECIFICATION. Pin the reasoning / inference-time-compute control
Sections affected: §5.6 (model configuration and version pinning); §7.3.1 inherits by reference

The gap in OUR protocol: §5.6 pins "model identifier, version, access date, temperature, and all
sampling parameters." A reasoning control is none of those. An extended-thinking toggle, a
reasoning-effort level, and a thinking-token budget are separate API-level operating modes, not
sampling parameters — a reader implementing this protocol exactly as written could leave the
setting unpinned, unreported, or drifting between runs and still be compliant.

Why that matters here specifically: Anchoring Failure Rate is a named secondary endpoint (§4.2)
and it carries this study's novelty claim. BiasMedQA shows that within a single model family the
reasoning setting moves cognitive-bias susceptibility, and moves it in different directions for
different models. An unpinned reasoning control is therefore an uncontrolled moderator sitting
directly on the endpoint the study is least able to afford noise on.

This is a specification defect that exists TODAY and is independent of whether BiasMedQA's
particular effect sizes replicate. It is the highest-priority item in this review.

Note on reach: §7.3.1 pins the LLM jury "as in §5.6". Because this edit adds the requirement inside
§5.6, the jury layer inherits it without a second edit — the three cross-vendor judges must also be
run at a single declared reasoning setting. One anchored change, both layers covered.

