EDIT 2 — EVIDENCE BALANCE. Add a peer-reviewed positive to the DECISION 1 note,
and correct an inference the note currently makes
Sections affected: §0 DECISION 1 evidence note

Two things are wrong with the note as it stands, and neither is a factual error:

(a) Evidentiary asymmetry. The positive side is one peer-reviewed study (Omar et al.) plus two
    preprints (MTS-Bench, MedMisBench). Both nulls (Jaarsma; Jia) are peer-reviewed. A reader
    working down the note sees the best-evidenced and most recent items all landing on the null
    side. BiasMedQA is peer-reviewed, PubMed-indexed, PMC-deposited, 1,273 vignettes, seven
    biases, three model families, mixed-effects regression — the methodologically strongest single
    item on the positive side.

(b) An inference that does not survive testing. The note tempers Jaarsma by observing that
    anchoring resistance "has been shown only for a newest-generation model." That is true as
    stated, but it carries an implication — that model advancement buys anchoring resistance —
    which BiasMedQA tests directly via matched reasoning variants and does not support. Correcting
    this makes the note's own reasoning sounder, and it does so in the direction of keeping
    Change 12 alive.

