EDIT 3 — JUSTIFICATION. Give §5.3 the measured magnitude it currently lacks
Sections affected: §5.3 (contamination controls)

To be clear about what is NOT being claimed: §5.3 is not deficient. De novo authorship, embedded
contamination-detection probes, a public/private split, a deliberate-overfitting demonstration, and
API access without web or tool access together make it one of the better-specified sections in the
protocol. Nothing here proposes changing a single control.

What it lacks is a number. The section currently justifies the private split on PROPAGATION
evidence — the Ramaswamy vignette set reused by two further evaluations within six months. That
establishes that published sets spread; it does not establish what the spreading costs. BiasMedQA
supplies the missing half from inside one study: the same model, published prompts versus
unpublished prompts.

The bound is stated in the proposed text and must stay there. It is one model on four items; the
authors themselves attribute the gap to contamination AND brittleness together and do not separate
them; and the unpublished range is wide (47.4-86.1%). It is not a clean contamination estimate and
the protocol should not present it as one. It is the closest published measurement of the quantity
the public/private split exists to protect, and it gives the embedded probes a pre-registered
expectation to be read against instead of a bare hope that memorization will show up.

